ackSegment`, allowing `is_entrypoint` to be set. The second-parent entrypoint case is covered by [with_workspace.rs]([REDACTED:path]).  \n  `s.id != stack_top_sidx` is redundant because the stop callback never receives the starting segment.\n\n- **Local branch boundary — keep as a split.**  \n  This converts one linear path into dependent branch segments. The current four-way matches can become simply “candidate has a user-visible local branch”; `stack_segment.ref_info` and `start.ref_info` do not affect the result.\n\n- **Internal `gitbutler/*` refs — keep as “do not split.”**  \n  These refs are graph-visible implementation details and should remain transparent to the user-facing projection. This is covered by [special-branch tests]([REDACTED:path]).\n\n- **Anonymous sibling boundary and reconnection exception — keep.**  \n  This supports the current `commits_outside`[REDACTED:path] reconstruction. The anonymous segment must begin a new `StackSegment` so it can inherit its sibling’s identity. Conversely, the exception at [init.rs]([REDACTED:path]) avoids splitting when that sibling is already in the collected path.\n\n- **Don’t continue into another direct workspace parent — keep as ownership logic.**  \n  Each direct workspace parent is collected independently. This guard prevents an ancestral workspace parent from also being appended to another parent’s stack. It is needed for the existing non-overlapping `Stack` projection, although it does not determine overall workspace membership.\n\n- **Parent-order hints — keep.**  \n  `StackSegment` is explicitly a first-parent-linearized projection, and the hints choose the parent that reaches the entrypoint through a merge. They are presentation-path selection, not a membership boundary.\n\n## Cleanup and discard conditions\n\n- **Start already at/below target → empty shell — keep temporarily.**  \n  Clearing the commits preserves metadata-backed branches as empty insertion points. Conceptually, the collector should return an empty path and projection should materialize the branch shell separately, but the current behavior is a reasonable bridge.\n\n- **`remove_empty_segments()` — keep.**  \n  This is structural cleanup after membership has already been determined. It retains applied metadata branches and removes anonymous empty shells.\n\n- **Lower-bound `discard_stack` callback — remove.**  \n  [This callback]([REDACTED:path]) is another old membership rule. Target-bounded collection plus empty-shell cleanup already handles target cases; without a target it wrongly hides reachable history.\n\n- **Post-collection “inactive and begins at `lowest_base`” filter — remove.**  \n  [This filter]([REDACTED:path]) encodes the same lower-bound model after traversal. Applied/unapplied empty branches should be decided by explicit metadata cleanup, not by whether their first commit happens to equal a computed merge base.\n\n- **Initial non-local `is_pruned` gate — likely remove separately.**  \n  It does not fit the traversal model and appears redundant under current graph construction: remote refs are placed on separate graph nodes while remote-only workspace paths become anonymous. I would delete it in a focused follow-up with its four remote/entrypoint regression scenarios.\n\n## The cleaner shape\n\n```text\nmanaged: for each workspace parent\nad-hoc:  once from HEAD\n\npath = collect_until_target(start, target_commit)\nstack_segments = partition_for_projection(path)\nremove_empty_structural_segments()\n```\n\n`collect_until_target()` would know only target ancestry, graph exhaustion, and cycle safety. Entrypoint, local-ref, special-ref, and sibling behavior would partition the returned path rather than repeatedly stopping and restarting traversal.\n\nTwo caveats:\n\n1. This is still a selected first-parent rail, not literal all-parent Git `parent ^target`. That is appropriate for the legacy [`StackSegment` contract]([REDACTED:path]), but not for a general workspace-membership subgraph.\n2. [Reconciliation]([REDACTED:path]) deliberately asks for paths before target filtering. It should get an explicitly separate raw/reconciliation collector instead of sharing `workspace_stacks()` with `None` as a hidden second traversal model.\n\nSo my deletion order would be:\n\n1. Remove both lower-bound stops, `has_seen_base`, and the ad-hoc exception.\n2. Remove the lower-bound discard and post-filter.\n3. Simplify the dead match inputs and entrypoint check.\n4. Separate path collection from presentation partitioning.\n5. Remove `is_pruned` in a focused cleanup.\n\nThis evaluation was read-only; I did not change the existing uncommitted traversal edit.\n\n<oai-mem-citation>\n<citation_entries>\nMEMORY.md:38-54|note=[target commit membership boundary and regression context]\nrollout_summaries/2026-07-14T19-57-06-t8iD-workspace_membership_head_minus_target_commit_boundary.md:15-27|note=[prior target boundary and cleanup rationale]\nrollout_summaries/2026-07-14T11-43-16-gmim-workspace_boundary_target_commit_id_vs_target_ref.md:17-32|note=[membership and integration status separation]\n<[REDACTED:path]>\n<rollout_ids>\n[REDACTED:entropy]\n[REDACTED:entropy]\n<[REDACTED:path]>\n<[REDACTED:path]>","tool_name":null,"tool_input":null,"source_record":{"timestamp":"2026-07-15T11:29:07.555Z","type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","id":"[REDACTED:entropy]","role":"assistant","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"[REDACTED:entropy]"},"phase":"final_answer"}}}